// PROCESS: separate memory. Two JVMs don't share heap objects.
//   Process A: java -jar app.jar     <- its own heap
//   Process B: java -jar app.jar     <- its own heap; no shared objects
//   To share data they need IPC (sockets, files, queues).

// THREAD: shares the process's memory (heap).
Runnable shared = () -> {
  // runs on a new thread but sees the SAME `list` as the main thread.
  list.add("from thread " + Thread.currentThread().getName());
};
new Thread(shared).start();
list.add("from main");
//   Easy sharing, but list is not thread-safe -> needs synchronization.
```
